167b6452cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62a31be5-1f88-5fce-bc3d-c7a604a6e638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511502 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2}) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}-{j}^{2})] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-{1}^{2})+({i}^{2}-{2}^{2})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(({i}^{2}-1)+({i}^{2}-4)) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(2{i}^{2}-5) & =(2(1)^{2}-5)+(2(2)^{2}-5)+(2(3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-3+3+13 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d3ae80f-587f-5674-9679-5efb33bf0d2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511507 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8da6073f-aed1-5284-a635-95231dda9375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511527 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1071 "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬P∧Q)∨(R∧Q)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d785d990-0971-50ad-9ff0-c1525a2beb2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511559 #edb/ref 1074 "We can factor out the conjunction with $Q,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬P∧Q)∨(R∧Q)≡(¬P∨R)∧Q\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27b93922-d5e3-56f6-9165-297356e94309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "282fa0dd-941b-516f-838f-7d24582c01b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ba69236-63ce-5e86-bc2d-fc9e6a6ed08f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511607 #edb/ref 1